INT. LAKESIDE BOATHOUSE - LATE AFTERNOON

A weathered boathouse leaning toward the water. Cedar planks gone silver. A rowing shell hangs from the rafters on rope slings, pale as a bone. Light slants through gaps in the boards and lies across the floor in long bars.

Dust turns in the light. A radio, tuned between stations, hisses faintly from the workbench.

THEODOSIA "DOSIA" WELLINGTON-ADEBAYO (60s, a man's cardigan over a housedress, hair tied up with a bootlace) sits on an overturned bucket, sanding the oar of a long-dead husband. She works with patient, practiced strokes. She has done this every autumn for thirty years.

ELIAS WELLINGTON-ADEBAYO (30s, city coat, rental-car keys on his finger) stands at the threshold. He has not stepped inside. His shoes are wrong for the planks.

ELIAS
Mama.

DOSIA
You're late.

ELIAS
Traffic.

DOSIA
There's no traffic on the lake road.

ELIAS
There was a deer.

She sands. The oar's grain comes up gold under her thumb.

DOSIA
You came to talk about the house.

ELIAS
I came to see you.

DOSIA
Yes. And to talk about the house.

He steps in at last. The floorboards give a long, low groan. He looks at the shell above him. Reaches up. Does not quite touch it.

ELIAS
It's still here.

DOSIA
Where would it go?

ELIAS
Dad would've sold it.

DOSIA
Your father would have rowed it into the lake and kept going.

ELIAS
Mama.

DOSIA
I'm only saying.

She finally looks up. Studies his face. His coat. His keys.

DOSIA (CONT'D)
You look like a man who's been told to be gentle.

ELIAS
Ruth thinks--

DOSIA
Ruth thinks a great many things from three states away.

ELIAS
She thinks you should come stay with us.

DOSIA
In an apartment.

ELIAS
A good apartment.

DOSIA
Elias.

ELIAS
Mama, the stairs. The pipes. Winter.

She sets down the sandpaper. Brushes dust off her knee with a slow hand.

DOSIA
Tell me, did you ever once, as a boy, carry that oar?

ELIAS
Once.

DOSIA
And?

ELIAS
I dropped it.

DOSIA
Into the lake.

ELIAS
Dad had to swim for it.

DOSIA
He was furious.

ELIAS
He was laughing.

DOSIA
Both things can be true.

The radio hisses. A gull cries somewhere over the water. Elias sinks onto a coil of rope, and the boathouse tilts a little, and holds.

A WOMAN's voice calls from the dock outside, bright and brisk.

RUTH (O.S.)
Elias? Is she in there?

Elias closes his eyes. Dosia's mouth thins. She picks up the sandpaper.

DOSIA
(low)
You brought her.

ELIAS
She drove. I didn't know--

DOSIA
Of course you didn't.

RUTH MARCHETTI-OSEI (30s, parka, a folder under her arm) steps into the doorway, silhouetted against the lake. She sees the shell above them, the oar in Dosia's lap, and the quiet. She stops.

RUTH
Oh. I'm interrupting.

DOSIA
Yes.

RUTH
I'll wait outside.

DOSIA
You'll come in. And you'll stand where I can see you.

Ruth does. The three of them make an awkward triangle on the creaking floor. The light bars stretch across their feet.

DOSIA (CONT'D)
Now. Which of you is going to tell me the real reason you're here?

Neither answers. The radio hisses between stations.

A small figure appears at the water door at the back: PEREGRINE "PERRY" ADEKUNLE-STRAND (10, wet to the knees, a bucket of minnows). He stops, takes in the three adults, and decides to be invisible.

DOSIA (CONT'D)
Perry. Put the minnows in the trough.

PERRY
Yes, Miss Dosia.

He tiptoes past them to the trough, pours, and tiptoes out the way he came.

DOSIA (CONT'D)
(softly)
Then I'll finish the oar.

She resumes sanding. Slow, rhythmic, patient. Elias watches his mother's hands. Ruth watches Elias. And the shell hangs above them like a held breath.
